        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Anecdotal and empirical findings collected in space and other extreme environments continue to highlight the potential for psychological symptoms and conditions to degrade crew performance, increase conflict, and jeopardize mission success. Indeed, ‘negative reactions’ during periods of isolation, confinement, demanding work schedules, stimulus reduction, separation from loved ones, sleep deprivation, and a host of other stressors are more appropriately viewed as normative rather than pathogenic. Selection methods and countermeasures serve to mitigate some degree of psychological risk, but long-duration space flight will substantially extend exposure to these and other stressors. Previous research documenting psychological symptoms experienced during space flight and in other isolated and confined environments (ICE) provides evidence of a wide range of psychological and behavioral reactions. Unfortunately, however, these collective data ultimately serve to raise more questions than answers. Differences in collection methods, types of symptoms/reactions assessed, psychological constructs examined, and timing and duration of measurements limit conclusions that can be drawn from this research. As a result, understanding of the discrete symptoms and conditions most likely to occur during space flight and thus, ability to quantify the magnitude, probability, or consequences of such risk remains inadequate. 
<div><br></div><div>
APPROACH:<br>
The study examined these notable gaps in knowledge via three specific aims. First, investigators conducted extensive scientific literature reviews and interviews with subject matter experts in order to synthesize existing knowledge of the psychological and behavioral symptoms experienced in space and other extreme environments (Aim 1). The review directly informed the development of a comprehensive checklist of symptoms to be monitored among 8 separate cohorts (i.e., 4 Antarctic and 4 HERA cohorts) as part of a longitudinal investigation (Aim 2). Symptoms were examined based on their point/period prevalence, severity, and duration. The checklist was also administered (repeatedly) in conjunction with the Space Medicine Exploration Medical Condition List (SMEMCL) in order to examine concurrent and sequential overlap between psychological and physical health symptoms as means of clarifying potential etiologies. Finally, this study extended previous research by exploring relationships among psychological health, sleep loss/dysregulation, biomarkers of stress, and performance-based outcomes (Aim 3). A comprehensive battery of cognitive and performance measures, including a perception-action coupling task, was administered repeatedly as part of the longitudinal study. These outcomes informed a final list of psychological/ behavioral symptoms to be examined during an extended International Space Station (ISS) mission.
</div><div><br></div><div>
RESULTS:<br><div>The results from this study indicated that Mental Health
Checklist (MHCL) is a reliable and useful measure for assessing psychological
risk in isolated, confined, extreme environments (ICE) settings. Each of the
MHCL scales contributed unique information, particularly when combined with
assessment of physical complaints. <o:p></o:p></div>

<div>The team identified several limitations of prior research
including conflation of distinct mental health constructs, lack of attention to
positive emotional states, imprecise terms and definitions, and use of measures
not validated for ICE environments. In order to address these limitations, the
team developed and tested the utility of MHCL measure in several unique
cohorts, including one HERA campaign, three large community-based samples
including one cohort surveyed during the initial phase of the COVID-19 pandemic,
and four separate Antarctic cohorts. <o:p></o:p></div>

<div>The findings among four separate Antarctic cohorts revealed
the MHCL positive adaptation subscale was most reliably impacted to the
prolonged stressors encountered. In the 9-month longitudinal study at the McMurdo
and South Pole stations, continuous declines in positive adaptation were
observed, even as crew members prepared to return home. Participants from the
longitudinal Antarctic study underwent emergency evacuation from the McMurdo
station during their participation. Monthly reports of psychological and
physical symptoms among four evacuees were compared to the remaining McMurdo
sample to identify unique indicators of distress prior to evacuation. Results
showed that emergency evacuation occurred for medical reasons for psychiatric
reasons. <o:p></o:p></div>

<div>The data included from HERA was collected during the 2016
HERA campaign. MHCL data was collected for each participant at each point and
the data was collected from N=16 participants. Greatest variability in positive
item scores was noted among participants across mission. This suggested that changes
in positive emotions were more sensitive to cumulative stress. The greatest
change was observed in positive adaptation scores compared to the other scales
following a night of total sleep deprivation. <o:p></o:p></div>

<div>In the community-samples, the validation study was conducted
to examine the 50-item MHCL. Exploratory factor analysis was conducted among a
diverse sample of N=300 undergraduate students at University of Houston.
Confirmatory factor analysis was conducted in a separate mixed community and
undergraduate sample (N=308). This included participants from United Kingdom
and United States. Three reliable subscales were formed based on the 23 items
identified. A 53% total variance in MHCL scores was seen for the 3 subscales:
positive adaptation, poor self-regulation and anxious apprehension.<o:p></o:p></div>

<div>In the absence of previous research, in mission use of
emotion regulatory strategies was also assessed in relation to changes in
positive adaptation. Greater use of suppression during the mission was
associated with low positive adaptation at the end of the mission. This
suggested counter strategies aimed at increasing experience and expression of
positive experiences that may help mitigate psychological risk.&nbsp;<o:p></o:p></div></div><div></div>
        </div>

